   271	## §3 The two boundary calls
   272	
   273	### 3.1 `:vm/store-update` is excluded from the canonical grammar
   274	
   275	The node carries a host function in its `:fn` field and applies it inside
   276	the syntax (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`). It is excluded on two grounds, and
   277	they are sufficient: a canonical object admits no host value (§2.2,
   278	`data`), and the node is absent from every persistent and portable form
   279	already, since the codec has no arm and throws on it (`v2.cljc:413-478`),
   280	the linearizer lists it as unsupported (`linearize.cljc:19-22`), the
   281	semantic VM has no opcode for it, and no test constructs one (a search of
   282	`test/` for the node type on 2026-09-14 found none). Its one reachable path
   283	is the walker's map-input arm, which survives only as long as that path
   284	(§9.1).
   285	
   286	The orchestrator's alternative, a `:fn` slot restricted to a named
   287	primitive symbol resolved and profile-checked per UCF §7.5.2, is not
   288	adopted: it would re-admit a node that no persistent form carries, for a
   289	convenience no corpus program uses.
   290	
   291	A program that needs the behaviour writes it as an application of the
   292	`yin/def` primitive (`v2.cljc:128`) over a `:vm/store-get` operand:
   293	`(yin/def k (f (vm/store-get k) args…))`. That rewrite is equivalent to
   294	the arm **only under stated conditions**, and this document does not claim
   295	it as a general semantics-preserving migration:
   296	
   297	- `yin/def` and `f` resolve through `resolve-var`'s precedence, env → store
   298	  → primitives → modules (`src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc:46-58`), so the
   299	  rewrite is equivalent only when neither name is shadowed at the site;
   300	- the arm stores whatever `(apply f current args)` returns, as data
   301	  (`ast_walker.cljc:410-415`), while an application interprets an
   302	  effect-shaped return (`ast_walker.cljc:184-188`,
   303	  `semantic.cljc:206-220`). An effect descriptor is itself plain data:
   304	  `module/effect?` recognizes any map carrying `:effect`
   305	  (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc:77-79`), and
   306	  `{:effect :vm/store-put :key :x :val 4}` passes `plain-data?`. So the
   307	  rewrite is equivalent only when `f` returns plain data **that is not an
   308	  effect descriptor under the applicable execution contract**; a result
   309	  the contract would interpret is stored by the arm and executed by the
   310	  rewrite.
   311	
   312	### 3.2 `:stream/close` exists, in both codec and walker
   313	
   314	One grammar truth: the instruction is real. The codec writes it
   315	(`v2.cljc:465-467`) and reads it back (`v2.cljc:549-550`), the linearizer
   316	lowers it (`linearize.cljc:135-136`), `yin.vm.code/mnemonics` admits
   317	`:stream-close` (`src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc:12-16`), the semantic VM
   318	decodes it (`semantic.cljc:585`) and executes it in its hot loop
   319	(`semantic.cljc:397-399`), and the engine handles the effect
   320	(`engine.cljc:480-482`). Only the walker lacks the arm
   321	(`ast_walker.cljc:359-469`).
   322	
   323	The walker gains it: evaluate `source`, then raise `{:effect :stream/close
   324	:stream ref}` through `engine/handle-effect`, through the
   325	`:eval-stream-close-source` frame of §2.6, shaped exactly as
   326	`:eval-stream-cursor-source` (`ast_walker.cljc:319-326`). Removing the
   327	instruction instead would delete working behaviour from the default
   328	evaluator to match the non-default one; the parity suite is the reason the
   329	two evaluators share one corpus.
