   333	## §4 Addressing
   334	
   335	### 4.1 The grain
   336	
   337	The address of a tree is `(dao.jing/segment-key tree)` computed over the
   338	whole canonical nested value (`jing.cljc:217-225`). A tree's address is the
   339	merkle grain the protocol pins: every named payoff below works at this
   340	grain and none needs a finer one.
   341	
   342	| Payoff | How it works at the whole-tree grain |
   343	|---|---|
   344	| `:yin.code/derived-from` as a content address | a derivation record naming the tree address as input and the segment address as output (§8.2) replaces the entity ref of `v2.cljc:327` and `linearize.cljc:212` |
   345	| the macro ledger | an expansion event naming input, output, and macro addresses (§8.4): expansion is a function from address to address, and each attempt is its own event |
   346	| module manifests pinning AST content | a manifest names the tree addresses it exports; equal content is equal address across hosts |
   347	| lowering as an address-to-address function | `lower : tree → instruction vector`, and each application of it is one derivation record (§5.2) |
   348	
   349	The address is deterministic and operand-order-sensitive: `(add 1 2)` and
   350	`(add 2 1)` address differently, because `order-normalize` sorts maps and
   351	sets but leaves vectors in place (`jing.cljc:45-64`). Literal maps and sets
   352	inside a `data` slot hash order-insensitively, which is correct: they are
   353	values.
   354	
   355	### 4.2 The transitional encoder is inherited, and every identity use is blocked on it
   356	
   357	`segment-key` hashes an order-normalized `pr-str` (`jing.cljc:67-71`,
   358	`:203-214`). `order-normalize` coerces **every** sequential value to a
   359	vector (`jing.cljc:63`), and `pr-str` prints no metadata. The encoder is
   360	therefore not injective over the `data` domain, and the collisions are not
   361	confined to one collection type:
   362	
   363	| Two different values | One address today | Why it matters |
   364	|---|---|---|
   365	| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (1 2)]` | lists become vectors | `conj` appends to one and prepends to the other |
   366	| `[:literal [1 2]]` and `[:literal (seq [1 2])]` | a seq is sequential but not a list | the same, and a list reject-rule does not see it |
   367	| `[:literal ^{:a 1} [1 2]]` and `[:literal ^{:a 2} [1 2]]` | metadata is dropped by `pr-str` | `data` admits plain metadata (`linearize.cljc:58`) and programs may read it |
   368	
   369	This is `dao.jing.md`'s first open item (*Canonical encoding*), inherited
   370	unchanged, and it is an acceptance blocker of this design (§10.1).
   371	
   372	**Every identity and deduplication use is blocked until the encoding is
   373	fixed.** No restricted value domain is claimed, and none is admitted: a
   374	reject-list over collection types does not make address-based identity
   375	safe, because the admitted domain is open (`plain-data?` admits every
   376	`coll?`, `linearize.cljc:56`) and the encoder's losses are structural. The
   377	block applies uniformly to every addressed object and every use of an
   378	address as identity: `data` slots of trees, `:const` and `:store-put`
   379	operands of vectors, ledger-record maps (§8.2), flat-projection row
   380	collapse (§6.1), DAG sharing (§4.4), process-local address-keyed caches,
   381	`dao.jing` reads that resolve an address to a literal object, and every
   382	ledger row that names an address. Process lifetime is irrelevant. Until
   383	the block lifts, addresses may be *computed* and *carried* for the
   384	conformance work of §7.2, but nothing may treat two equal addresses as
   385	one value.
   386	
   387	The three rows above are part of the **encoder conformance obligation**
   388	that lifts the block: the pinned encoding must address each pair
   389	distinctly, and the test that proves it is part of `dao.jing`'s
   390	acceptance, not this document's. When it lands, every address minted under
   391	this design changes with every other `dao.jing` address, the dependency
   392	UCF §7.3.2 already accepts.
   393	
   394	### 4.3 Per-node addresses and the per-lambda follow-up
   395	
   396	The flat projection (§6.1) keys each node by the address of its own
   397	inlined subtree, computed by the same `segment-key`. These per-node
   398	addresses are a consequence of the hash function applied recursively; they
   399	exist in the projection and nowhere in the canonical object, and nothing in
