   477	#### 5.2.1 The lowering profile
   478	
   479	UCF §7.3.3's execution-contract stamp versions the instruction grammar,
   480	the transitions, resolution precedence, effect outcomes, and scheduling.
   481	Its declared scope does not include the AST-to-instruction algorithm, and
   482	two deterministic lowerers can target one execution contract and emit two
   483	different, equally valid vectors. A derivation is therefore verifiable
   484	only against a **lowering profile**, a published revision that pins:
   485	
   486	| Pinned by the profile | Reference value for `"ast-v1"` |
   487	|---|---|
   488	| input grammar | the §2 tuple grammar, by revision of this document |
   489	| normalization applied before emission | saturation (§2.4), tail marks on `:application` only (§2.3), nothing else |
   490	| emission rules | the §5.3 flattening table of `yin.vm.semantic.md`, in the walker's evaluation order (operator, then operands left to right) |
   491	| label and body order | labels numbered in emission order (`linearize.cljc:76-77`); bodies emitted after the main sequence, in the order lambdas were encountered, including lambdas found inside bodies (`linearize.cljc:151-158`) |
   492	| terminators | main sequence ends in `:halt`, every body in `:return` (`linearize.cljc:150`, `:157`) |
   493	| target execution contract | the UCF §7.3.3 stamp the emitted grammar must satisfy |
   494	
   495	The profile is a dedicated stamp, not an extension of the execution
   496	contract: an execution contract may be targeted by several profiles, and a
   497	profile is revised whenever any pinned rule changes, whether or not the
   498	execution contract does. A lowering that introduces any host-dependent
   499	choice violates its profile and is a defect. The profile is the structured
   500	map above, carried **exactly** in the record and in the event projection
   501	(§8.2); no string form of it is defined and none is used.
   502	
   503	#### 5.2.2 Verification, in two separate steps
   504	
   505	A consumer holding a tree, a vector, and a derivation record verifies two
   506	different things and reports them separately:
   507	
   508	1. **Object integrity.** Each object hashes to the address it is claimed
   509	   under: `(segment-key tree)` equals `:yin.ledger/input`,
   510	   `(segment-key vector)` equals `:yin.ledger/output`. Failure is
   511	   `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` naming the object. This is UCF §7.3.4's check and
   512	   says nothing about the derivation.
   513	2. **Derivation.** If, and only if, the consumer implements the record's
   514	   `:yin.lower/profile` exactly, it recomputes `(segment-key (lower tree))`
   515	   under that profile and compares it with `:yin.ledger/output`. Equality
   516	   verifies the asserted derivation; inequality is `:yin.k/derivation-
   517	   mismatch`, a defective or dishonest lowering, never repaired silently. A
   518	   consumer implementing a different profile does not recompute: it reports
