   874	#### 7.7.2 Name obligations are per context, and completion is conservative
   875	
   876	A name extracted from code is an **obligation** of every activation that
   877	can execute that code, and an obligation is discharged only by a binding
   878	that activation would actually see under `resolve-var`'s precedence
   879	(`engine.cljc:46-65`). Resolution is activation-specific: the reference
   880	resolver returns the primitive for `x` under an empty environment and the
   881	environment's binding under one that binds `x`. So a binding discharges
   882	an obligation only in one of these contexts:
   883	
   884	| Obligation from | Discharged by |
   885	|---|---|
   886	| a `:variable` inside a `:lambda` body whose params, or an enclosing lambda's params on the structural path, bind the name | the lexical binding, statically, for that occurrence |
   887	| code the active control points into | the active frame's environment, or the store slice by key |
   888	| a closure's body (the segment its `:entry` points into) | that closure's captured `:env`, or the store slice |
   889	| code a K frame, a parked record, or a wait entry resumes into | that frame's or record's own `:env`, or the store slice |
   890	| code reachable only by address, with no activation associated | nothing: the obligation is retained |
   891	
   892	**An unrelated carried binding never discharges an obligation.** A saved
   893	continuation that binds `x` says nothing about the active activation's
   894	`x`, which may resolve to a primitive with a profile the value needs. Every
   895	retained obligation is a required primitive or module export, checked by
   896	profile (UCF §7.5.2), and the **effects of a callable are read from its
   897	profile** (`:yin.k/effects`), never inferred from the name: `yin/def`
   898	contributes `:vm/store-put` and `require` contributes `:module/require`
   899	because their profiles say so. Where the analysis cannot associate an
   900	occurrence with a context, or a retained name has no profile at the
   901	emitter, discovery is `:incomplete`; conservatively retaining every
   902	possible primitive and module requirement is always admissible and never
   903	makes a `:complete` result wrong.
   904	
   905	This rule is the amendment UCF §7.6.1's *Names* paragraph needs: its
   906	sentence that a name "is satisfied if it is bound in any environment the
   907	value carries" is the unsound test, and this document states the
   908	per-context rule in its place; UCF must be amended to say the same
   909	(§10.8).
