   938	## §8 The ledger and refs
   939	
   940	### 8.1 Two notations, one relation
   941	
   942	The ledger is one logical relation with rows `[t op address ref]`, ruling
   943	6's shape: `t` is the ledger's clock, `op` is drawn from an open
   944	vocabulary, `address` is a content address, and `ref` is the row's other
   945	argument, a name entity or a second address. This document writes ledger
   946	facts in that notation when it speaks of the relation.
   947	
   948	The **persistent representation** of a row is datoms, and a datom is bound
   949	by `datom.md`'s contract: `e`, `t`, and `m` are stream-local, `e` is a
   950	non-negative integer, `m` is an integer, and `v` is the only slot that may
   951	hold a foreign reference (`datom.md:140-180`). The transactor enforces
   952	this: `local-datom?` (`src/cljc/dao/datom.cljc:42-58`) rejects a
   953	non-integer `e` or `m`, and `pad-datom`
   954	(`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:74-90`) rejects a caller-supplied `t`
   955	and stamps its own. Therefore a content address never appears in `e`, an
   956	op keyword never appears in `m`, and `t` is never chosen by the writer.
   957	The mapping between notations is:
   958	
   959	| Logical slot | Persistent carrier |
   960	|---|---|
   961	| `t` | the datom `t` the transactor stamped |
   962	| `op` | for naming rows: the reserved validity marker in `m` (`:db/assert` 1, `:db/retract` 0); for every other op: the `:yin.ledger/op` value of a **ledger event entity** (§8.2) |
   963	| `address` | a `v` slot |
   964	| `ref` | for naming rows: the local name entity in `e`; for address-to-address rows: a second `v` slot of the same event entity |
   965	
   966	Anything written in the `[t op address ref]` notation below is a logical
   967	row; anything written as `[e a v t m]` is transactor input, with `e` a
   968	local integer or tempid, `t` nil at write time, and `m` a reserved marker.
   969	
   970	### 8.2 Ledger records and ledger events
   971	
   972	Every non-naming ledger fact is first a **ledger record**: a plain map,
   973	content-addressed by `segment-key`, stored in `dao.jing` like any object.
   974	Record shapes:
   975	
   976	| Op | Record | Meaning |
   977	|---|---|---|
   978	| `:derive` | `{:yin.ledger/op :derive :yin.ledger/input tree-addr :yin.ledger/output seg-addr :yin.ledger/function fn-name :yin.ledger/profile profile-map}` | `output` was computed from `input` by `function` under the lowering profile (§5.2.1) |
   979	| `:expand` | `{:yin.ledger/op :expand :yin.ledger/input tree-in :yin.ledger/call occurrence-key :yin.ledger/output tree-out :yin.ledger/macro lambda-addr :yin.ledger/attempt attempt-id}` | one expansion attempt; failures carry `:yin.ledger/error` instead of `:yin.ledger/output` (§8.4) |
   980	| `:publish` | `{:yin.ledger/op :publish :yin.ledger/subject occurrence-id :yin.ledger/output ucf-addr}` | a checkpoint occurrence was published as the UCF value at `output` (UCF §7.3.4) |
   981	
   982	A record is an object, so it is immutable, addressable, and verifiable,
   983	and it is what "the hash chain" is made of: a record names its input and
   984	output by address, and the record's own address is what the ledger
   985	publishes. Walking the chain is following record addresses; a code object
   986	never has to know its predecessor.
   987	
   988	A record enters the ledger as a **ledger event entity**, a local entity
   989	(id ≥ `dao.datom/first-user-id`, `datom.cljc:27-40`) with the record's
   990	fields as attributes and the record's address as one more:

