  1018	`src/cljc/dao/space/transact.cljc:15-29`). A fact whose `m` names a
  1019	metadata entity is live under the current fold, which removes only
  1020	`:db/retract` markers (`query.cljc:76-103`); a validity op other than
  1021	assert or retract is expressed by the reserved marker, never by the
  1022	event, so the fold needs no knowledge of the open vocabulary. Interpreting
  1023	a metadata entity's `:db/op` for Datomic-visible validity is the
  1024	prerequisite `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names and is outside this
  1025	design.
  1026	
  1027	The vocabulary is open: a composition that needs a new op declares a new
  1028	keyword and a record shape. A reader dispatches on the ops it understands
  1029	(`[?ev :yin.ledger/op :derive]`) and does not see the others, which is a
  1030	property of the query it writes, not of the fold.
  1031	
  1032	### 8.3 Rows of the one relation
  1033	
  1034	| Logical row | Persistent form |
  1035	|---|---|
  1036	| `[t :assert tree-addr name]` — a name refers to a tree | `[:db/add name :yin/code tree-addr]` |
  1037	| `[t :retract tree-addr name]` — the name no longer does | `[:db/retract name :yin/code tree-addr]`, followed by the new assertion |
  1038	| `[t :derive seg-addr tree-addr]` — a segment was lowered from a tree | a `:derive` event entity (§8.2); direction: `input` is the tree, `output` is the segment |
  1039	| `[t :assert seg-addr k-occurrence]` — a segment is the address a continuation names | `[:db/add occ :yin.k/segment seg-addr]` on the local occurrence entity |
  1040	| `[t :expand tree-out tree-in]` — a tree is the expansion of another | an `:expand` event entity (§8.4) |
  1041	| `[t :assert lambda-addr name]` under `:yin.macro/definition` | not a ledger row at all: macro-ness is an occurrence-bound declaration beside the tree (§8.5); the ledger records only the expander's harvest result, `[:db/add name :yin.macro/definition lambda-addr]`, for queries |
  1042	| `[t :publish ucf-addr occurrence]` — a checkpoint occurrence was published | a `:publish` event entity; custody stays `dao.lease.md`'s vocabulary unchanged (UCF §7.7.2), keyed by the same local occurrence entity |
  1043	| `[t :assert tree-addr manifest]` — a module manifest pins a tree | `[:db/add manifest :yin.module/exports tree-addr]` |
  1044	
  1045	`:yin.code/derived-from` therefore keeps its name as a query alias for
  1046	"the `:yin.ledger/input` of a `:derive` event whose output is this
  1047	address" and is no longer an attribute of the segment (`v2.cljc:327`,
  1048	`linearize.cljc:212`).
  1049	
  1050	### 8.4 Expansion events keep their identity
  1051	
  1052	`yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1 records one event per attempt, successful or not,
  1053	including repeated identical expansions, and links the event to its output
  1054	(`yin.vm.macro.md:666-709`). This design keeps that contract: the
  1055	`:expand` event entity **is** that event, one per attempt, and the address
  1056	rows are attributes of it, not a replacement for it:
  1057	
  1058	```clojure
  1059	[[:db/add ev :yin.ledger/op        :expand]
  1060	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/input     tree-in-addr]        ; the tree containing the call
  1061	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/call      [origin tree-in-addr path]]   ; the occurrence of the call (§2.5)
  1062	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/output    tree-out-addr]       ; on success
  1063	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/macro     lambda-addr]         ; the macro's content
  1064	 [:db/add ev :yin.ledger/attempt   n]                   ; per-expander attempt counter
  1065	 [:db/add ev :yin/source-batch     batch-id]            ; unchanged from §4.1
  1066	 [:db/add ev :yin/error            {...}]               ; on failure, unchanged
  1067	 [:db/add ev :yin/timestamp        ctx-t]]              ; unchanged
  1068	```
  1069	
  1070	The `origin` inside `:yin.ledger/call` is what disambiguates ancestry, and
  1071	it follows `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.1's own split (`yin.vm.macro.md:692-708`):
  1072	
  1073	- an **initial** expansion's call sits in admitted syntax, so its origin is
  1074	  `[:source medium batch]`, the successor of `:yin/source-call` qualified
  1075	  by `:yin/source-batch`;
  1076	- a **nested** expansion's call sits in the output of the enclosing
  1077	  expansion, so its origin is `[:expansion enclosing-ev]`, the successor of
  1078	  `:yin/source-node`: a local ref to the enclosing event, resolved within
  1079	  the log medium exactly as `:yin/source-node` is today.
  1080	
  1081	A successful event carries exactly one of the two origins; an admission
  1082	failure carries neither. The chain `source → event → output → event →
  1083	output` of §4.1 is therefore walked through event ids, and two attempts
  1084	that produced identical output trees are two events with two ids, so a
  1085	later `[:expansion ev]` names its parent without ambiguity.
  1086	`:yin/expansion-root` and `:yin/macro` of §4.1 are carried by
  1087	`:yin.ledger/output` and `:yin.ledger/macro` as addresses; the other event
  1088	attributes are unchanged. Because the *content* values are addresses, the
  1089	cross-media identity problem of `yin.vm.macro.md` §4.2 does not arise for
  1090	content: the same tree has the same address on `program-in`,
  1091	`program-out`, and the log. Occurrence coordinates remain log-local, as
  1092	`:yin/source-node` is today, which is the correct scope for them.
  1093	
  1094	### 8.5 Macro declarations travel beside the tree
  1095	
  1096	The `:macro?` flag is outside the canonical tree (§2.5). What replaces it
  1097	is a **declaration fact bound to an occurrence**, produced by the frontend
  1098	and consumed by the expander at admission. The governing contract admits
  1099	and harvests **every entity in the batch, in batch order, including
  1100	definitions disconnected from the execution root**
  1101	(`yin.vm.macro.md:229-249`). A single rooted tree cannot carry a
  1102	disconnected definition, so the composition between frontend and expander
  1103	is an **ordered batch**, not a tree:
  1104	
  1105	```clojure
  1106	{:yin/batch         [<tree-0> <tree-1> … <tree-n>]   ; canonical trees, in batch order
  1107	 :yin/root          i                                ; index of the tree the evaluator runs
  1108	 :yin/declarations  #{[j path :yin.macro/definition] …}}   ; the definition at path in tree j is a macro
  1109	```
  1110	
  1111	Rules:
  1112	
  1113	- **Batch order is preserved.** `:yin/batch` is a vector; its order is
  1114	  the admitted order, and a tree at any index may be a definition the root
  1115	  never reaches. The datom codec's batch order (`v2.cljc:372-482` emits in
  1116	  conversion order) maps to vector order, and within one tree harvest
  1117	  order is preorder of paths. This preserves the whole-batch, batch-order
  1118	  harvest of `yin.vm.macro.md` §3.1 step 2 exactly, including
  1119	  disconnected definitions; the old contract is **not** narrowed.
  1120	- **Binding.** A declaration `[j path :yin.macro/definition]` names the
  1121	  `:application` at `path` in tree `j`; that node must be
  1122	  `(yin/def <literal sym> <lambda>)`, i.e. operator `[:variable yin/def]`,
  1123	  first operand a `:literal` symbol, second operand a `:lambda`. A
  1124	  declaration whose coordinates do not resolve to such a node is an
  1125	  admission failure, `{:kind :malformed-input :reason
  1126	  :stray-macro-declaration}`, the successor of §3.1 step 1's
  1127	  `:stray-macro-lambda`.
  1128	- **Marks are validated before they are stripped.** A frontend or the
  1129	  §9.1 adapter that converts a map AST carrying `:macro? true` must reject
  1130	  a flagged lambda anywhere but the value operand of a `yin/def` as
  1131	  `:stray-macro-lambda` **at conversion**, before the flag is removed;
  1132	  it may never strip such a flag and emit a plain lambda. A flag in the
  1133	  admitted position becomes the declaration at that tree and path. The
  1134	  quiet case `yin.vm.macro.md` decision 5 exists to prevent is therefore
  1135	  prevented at the same point it is today.
  1136	- **Ordering of harvest.** Trees in vector order, nodes in preorder within
  1137	  a tree; last wins, as §3.1 step 2 rules.
  1138	- **Missing fact.** A `(yin/def sym v)` with no declaration at its
  1139	  coordinates is a plain definition. If `sym` is currently a macro, the
  1140	  plain definition removes it, exactly as §3.1 step 2 rules.
  1141	- **Scope.** Harvest is syntactic and whole-batch, as today; a declaration
  1142	  in an unexecuted branch or in a tree the root never reaches is in force
  1143	  for the batch. Declarations are per batch and never persist beyond the
  1144	  harvest that consumed them; the harvested store is the expander's state.
  1145	- **Redefinition.** Two batches that carry identical trees differ in
  1146	  behaviour only through their declarations, which is the intended
  1147	  separation: content is what the program is; being a macro is what this
  1148	  occurrence of a definition is *for*.
  1149	- **Evaluators see the root only.** The composition hands an evaluator
  1150	  `(nth batch root)` after expansion; a tree outside the root is expander
  1151	  input, never program.
  1152	
  1153	This replacement protocol, not the expander itself, is the blocker
  1154	(§10.5): tuple evaluation without macros needs none of it, and the v2
  1155	expander does not exist yet.
  1156	
  1157	### 8.6 As-of queries are ledger views
  1158	
  1159	Current value of a name uses the implemented current-state interpreter,
  1160	which resolves the greatest `t` per `[e a v]` and then removes retractions
  1161	(`query/current`, `query.cljc:204-212`; the fold `current-state-seq`,
  1162	`query.cljc:76-103`):
  1163	
  1164	```clojure
  1165	(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]
  1166	   (query/current refs) 'my.ns/f)
  1167	```
  1168	
  1169	Value of a name as of `?t` is the same view with its as-of argument, which
  1170	bounds visible datoms to `t ≤ ?t` before folding
  1171	(`query/current` two-arity, `query.cljc:210-212`; `bound-datoms`,
  1172	`query.cljc:68-73`):
  1173	
  1174	```clojure
  1175	(q '[:find ?addr . :in $ ?name :where [?e :yin/name ?name] [?e :yin/code ?addr]]
  1176	   (query/current refs t) 'my.ns/f)
  1177	```
  1178	
  1179	A hand-written negation over raw d5 rows is not the contract and is not
  1180	used: "any retraction before `?t`" wrongly removes an address that was
  1181	retracted and later reasserted, whereas the fold's latest-event-wins per
  1182	`[e a v]` keeps it. A query that must inspect raw rows selects the latest
  1183	event per `[e a v]` with `max ?t` first and interprets that row's `m`
  1184	second.
  1185	
  1186	Provenance walks are joins over event entities:
  1187	
  1188	```clojure
  1189	;; what was this segment lowered from, and what did that expand from
  1190	[:find ?src
  1191	 :in $ ?seg
  1192	 :where [?d :yin.ledger/op :derive] [?d :yin.ledger/output ?seg] [?d :yin.ledger/input ?tree]
  1193	        [?x :yin.ledger/op :expand] [?x :yin.ledger/output ?tree] [?x :yin.ledger/input ?src]]
  1194	```
  1195	
  1196	---
  1197	
  1198	## §9 Migration surface
  1199	
  1200	### 9.1 The mechanical sweep
  1300	   value domain is claimed; the round-2 list-rejection rule is withdrawn
  1301	   as unsound. Trees, vectors, ledger-record maps, flat-projection
  1302	   collapse, DAG sharing, process-local caches, object resolution by
  1303	   address, and ledger rows are all blocked alike until `dao.jing`'s
  1304	   encoding passes the conformance pairs of §4.2. This design does not
  1305	   close the item.
  1306	2. **The store-key domain is the `data` domain** (§2.2): every validator,
  1307	   extraction query, and store-slice encoding must use that one
  1308	   definition. This document now states it uniformly; the implementation
  1309	   phase must not reintroduce a narrower one, and the corpus must contain
  1310	   a numeric key beside the keyword one.
  1311	3. **Complete occurrence identity** (§2.5, §5.3, §8.4): source origins
  1312	   `[:source medium batch]` and generated origins `[:expansion event-id]`
  1313	   must be supplied to the lowering and recorded in instruction
  1314	   provenance and expansion events. The medium identity coordinate is
  1315	   whatever the composition names its program medium by; this document
  1316	   does not fix its shape.
  1317	4. **Macro batch and admission preservation** (§8.5): the ordered-batch
  1318	   composition and the validate-before-strip rule must exist before any
  1319	   tuple program containing a macro definition can be admitted. The v2
  1320	   expander itself does not exist yet (`src/cljc/yin/vm/` has no
  1321	   `macro.cljc`) and is not a blocker for tuple evaluation without macros;
  1322	   §8.4's event shape is specified against `yin.vm.macro.md`, not against
  1323	   code.
  1324	5. **Conservative dependency completion** (§7.7.2): per-context
  1325	   obligations must be implemented as stated, and any occurrence the
  1326	   analysis cannot place must be retained, not discharged.
  1327	6. **Effect normalization** (§7.7.1): the footprint table must be
  1328	   published with the execution-contract stamp, and the tree/segment
  1329	   requirement-set equality must be tested over the corpus.
  1330	7. **The lowering profile** (§5.2.1): `"ast-v1"` must be published as a
  1331	   pinned document before any derivation record is written; until then
  1332	   no derivation can be verified, only object integrity (§5.2.2).
  1333	8. **UCF §7.6.1 needs the per-context amendment** (§7.7.2): its
  1334	   any-carried-environment satisfaction sentence is unsound and must be
  1335	   replaced by the rule stated here. This document cannot edit UCF.
  1336	9. **The observer-lane batch shape and the program-input predicate**
  1337	   (§9.2) — integration items with no design answer in this document. The
  1338	   §8.5 ordered batch is a candidate shape for the AST medium, not a
  1339	   verified one.
  1340	10. **Ledger event representation on the transactor** (§8.2): the
  1341	    event-entity form is legal under `local-datom?` as written, but the
  1342	    map-`m` provenance path (`transact.cljc:15-29`) and the metadata-op
  1343	    interpreter `dao.space.schema.datomic.md` §4 names are not exercised by
  1344	    this design's tests yet. The naming rows need neither; the provenance
  1345	    link from a naming fact to its event does.
  1346	11. **The primitive-profile registry** — inherited from UCF §7.11. Every
  1347	    retained name obligation is checked by profile under UCF §7.5.2, and
  1348	    §7.7.2 reads callable effects from profiles; until
  1349	    `yin.vm/primitives` (`v2.cljc:100-132`) is published with profiles,
  1350	    dependency closure reports names and `:incomplete`, not satisfiable
  1351	    bindings.
  1352	12. **Per-lambda subterm addressing** — a follow-up (§4.3), not a blocker.
