   811	
   812	The FFI pair is never in the slice — a program that names the pair's store
   813	keys directly holds handles, and the lift refuses it (§7.5.4). A resumer has
   814	its own pair; outstanding calls route per §7.4.3.
   815	
   816	**There is no merge.** The first draft said, in two places, both that the
   817	slice wins on collision and that the slice merges *under* the local store —
   818	a contradiction, and beneath it a real defect: resolution is env → store →
   819	primitives (`resolve-var`), so a resumer whose local store happens to bind
   820	`+` would change what the migrated program resolves, without any collision
   821	at all. Merging in either order is the wrong shape. **The resumed task runs
   822	in an isolated execution store**: a fresh store whose contents are exactly
   823	the carried slice, the remapped cells of §7.5.3, and the resumer's FFI pair
   824	— nothing else of the resumer's, nothing of any other task's. A name absent
   825	from the slice resolves through primitives and modules at the resumer
   826	exactly as it did at the emitter, because there is no third store to
   827	intervene; a slice key cannot collide with a receiver task's key because no
   828	receiver key participates; resource entries are remapped to fresh local
   829	keys (cursor cells per §7.5.3, stream handles per §7.4.3), and non-resource
   830	entries keep their keys verbatim. "Unreachable store entries do not travel"
   831	survives unchanged — anything the program did not observe is not the
   832	program's state — and nothing else shares state with the resumed task by
   833	accident.
   834	
