   229	1. **Admit.** The batch is indexed with `index-datoms` and checked as a
   230	   whole — **every entity in the index, not only those reachable from the
   231	   root**, because harvest (step 2) reads disconnected definitions: every
   232	   `:yin/type` is in the closed vocabulary (§3.3), every ref resolves to an
   233	   entity in the index, the whole graph is acyclic, every ground value is
   234	   plain data, and every `:macro? true` lambda is the value operand of a
   235	   `(yin/def <literal sym> …)` — a macro lambda anywhere else has no meaning
   236	   on either side of the boundary and, forwarded, would load on the walker
   237	   as a plain closure, the quiet case decision 5 exists to prevent — all
   238	   *before* any recursive decoding (Appendix B). An in-memory medium can
   239	   carry host values, and the sandbox's closed-input guarantee holds only if
   240	   nothing else is admitted. A failure here is
   241	   `{:status :error :error {:kind :malformed-input :reason :unknown-type |
   242	   :dangling-ref | :cyclic | :host-value | :stray-macro-lambda :eid e}}`
   243	   carrying a **batch-level
   244	   event** (§4.1) — allocated from the watermark alone, since the index may
   245	   be unusable — and the advanced `:ctx`. Diagnostics name entities, never
   246	   carry the offending values.
   247	2. **Harvest.** `definitions` finds every `(yin/def <literal sym> v)` in the
   248	   batch, in batch order, last wins: `v` a `:macro? true` lambda →
   249	   `assoc` into `:store`; `v` anything else (a plain lambda, a variable, a
   250	   literal) → `dissoc`, so a redefinition as a non-macro stops expansion of
